profile: ext_tags=on
# Custom tag names

Setting: `ext_tags`.

A div and a span can have a name, which becomes the element.

``` example
Press :kbd[Ctrl+C] to copy.
```

``` example
::: details
Hidden until opened.
:::
```

This extension changes how existing djot documents parse: in djot the word
after a div fence is a class, and `:name[` is text before a bracket.

## A div's name

The word after a div's opening fence is its name. Which lines open a div
does not change. A fence with no word opens an unnamed div.

``` example
{.note}
::: aside
A named div with a class.
:::
```

## A span's name

`:name[` opens a named span. The name is made of the characters a symbol
`:name:` may contain. The span closes at the first `]` that arrives while
it is the innermost open bracket, and nothing after the `]` is waited for.

``` example
:kbd[x](u)
```

Attributes attach as they do to any inline.

``` example
:kbd[Esc]{.key}
```

## Where `:name[` is not a named span

After an escaped colon, or when the colon directly follows a letter, a
digit or a colon.

``` example
\:kbd[a] and a:kbd[b]
```

When it never closes.

``` example
:kbd[a
```

## Output

The name is the element name when it is made of ASCII letters, digits and
hyphens, starts with a letter, and is not an element whose content a
browser reads differently (a void element such as `br`, or `script`,
`style`, `textarea`, `title`, `iframe` and similar). Otherwise the element
is `div` or `span` and the name is in a `data-tag` attribute.

``` example
:script[x] and :a_b[y]
```

{% ai-disclosure: ai-generated %}
